WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.004
Scenario 3 walkthrough: Multi-Agent Research System.

2
00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:16.201
Research systems fail quietly when a coordinator splits the work badly or a subagent hides an error, so this scenario tests how you delegate, pass context and keep sources attached.

3
00:00:17.601 --> 00:00:20.763
Scenario three is a multi agent research system.

4
00:00:20.763 --> 00:00:31.103
A coordinator agent takes a research question and hands parts of it to specialised subagents for web search, document analysis, synthesis and report writing.

5
00:00:31.103 --> 00:00:37.953
The output is a research report with citations, so every claim has to stay linked to where it came from.

6
00:00:39.353 --> 00:00:49.015
You will see coordinator and subagent patterns, how to pass context when spawning subagents, how to split the work, and which tools each agent should get.

7
00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:58.489
Domain five adds two important ideas: how errors travel back to the coordinator, and how to keep claims linked to their sources when findings conflict.

8
00:00:59.889 --> 00:01:01.106
Here is a classic.

9
00:01:01.106 --> 00:01:06.110
The final report on AI in creative industries only talks about visual art.

10
00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.476
Each subagent did its own job well.

11
00:01:08.476 --> 00:01:14.899
The root cause is the coordinator's decomposition, which never assigned music, writing or film.

12
00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:21.457
The fix is broader, non overlapping partitions, plus a coverage check before the final synthesis.

13
00:01:22.857 --> 00:01:24.041
Now a failure case.

14
00:01:24.041 --> 00:01:25.786
A search subagent times out.

15
00:01:25.786 --> 00:01:31.081
Returning an empty result hides the failure and looks like a real finding of nothing.

16
00:01:31.081 --> 00:01:34.134
Killing the whole workflow throws away good work.

17
00:01:34.134 --> 00:01:43.231
The right move is a structured error with the failure type, the query tried and any partial results, so the coordinator can retry or note the gap.

18
00:01:44.631 --> 00:01:46.199
Distractors to watch for.

19
00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:51.781
Assuming subagents share the coordinator's memory, when you must pass context explicitly.

20
00:01:51.781 --> 00:01:53.726
Handing every agent every tool.

21
00:01:53.726 --> 00:01:59.873
Blending conflicting figures into one number instead of showing both with their sources and dates.

22
00:01:59.873 --> 00:02:05.644
And running independent subagents one by one rather than in parallel from a single response.

23
00:02:07.044 --> 00:02:13.532
For scenario three, remember that missing coverage usually traces back to how the coordinator split the work.

24
00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:16.507
Subagents inherit nothing, so pass what they need.

25
00:02:16.507 --> 00:02:20.852
Errors should travel with their type, what was tried and partial results.

26
00:02:20.852 --> 00:02:27.161
And the synthesis must keep every claim tied to its source, showing disagreements rather than hiding them.

